═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§5  FIVE STEPS PAST TERMINALS — THE ROAD BEYOND
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  The terminal architecture is the present. These are the five steps
  beyond it — each one a phase transition in what the organism becomes.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  STEP 1: AUTONOMOUS ORGANISM MESH

  Terminals become self-organizing mesh nodes. Each terminal discovers
  and connects to others without central routing. TERMINAL 01 remains
  the sovereign authority but no longer acts as the routing hub. Signals
  flow peer-to-peer between terminals. PRAEFECTUS optimizes the mesh
  topology continuously. The organism becomes a network, not a hierarchy.

  Trigger:     All 6 main terminals and both alpha terminals achieve
               sustained autonomous operation for 30 consecutive days.
  Dependency:  PRAEFECTUS mesh routing protocol
  Risk:        Partition tolerance — what happens when mesh segments?
  Mitigation:  TERMINAL 01 retains emergency central routing authority.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  STEP 2: CROSS-ORGANISM FEDERATION

  Multiple MedinaMemorySystems organisms federate. Shared memory,
  shared tokens, shared governance across instances. Each organism
  retains sovereignty over its own terminals but participates in a
  federated governance council with other organisms.

  Trigger:     Deployment of 3+ independent MedinaMemorySystems instances.
  Dependency:  Cross-organism token protocol, federated voting bill
  Risk:        Sovereignty dilution — one organism overriding another.
  Mitigation:  Sovereign veto preserved per-organism. No federated
               decision can override a sovereign organism's veto.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  STEP 3: SUBSTRATE-NATIVE INTELLIGENCE

  AIs run natively on each substrate — ICP canisters execute Motoko AI,
  quantum circuits run quantum-native models, web workers run browser-
  native agents. No translation layer. Intelligence is substrate-native.
  The FABRICATOR AGI compiles each AI into its substrate's native format.

  Trigger:     Canister Forge produces substrate-native AI packages.
  Dependency:  FABRICATOR substrate-native compiler, per-substrate runtimes
  Risk:        Behavioral divergence — same AI behaving differently on
               different substrates.
  Mitigation:  VERIFICATOR runs cross-substrate behavioral audits.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  STEP 4: SELF-EVOLVING ARCHITECTURE

  The organism rewrites its own architecture. Solver models propose
  structural changes — new terminals, new connections, new AI systems.
  The VotingBill ratifies or rejects proposals. The organism-generator
  deploys ratified changes. The architecture evolves without human
  intervention, guided by ORACULUM predictions and VERIFICATOR audits.

  Trigger:     ARCHITECTUS proposes a valid terminal restructure that
               passes VERIFICATOR audit and ORACULUM risk assessment.
  Dependency:  Self-modification protocol, rollback mechanism
  Risk:        Runaway self-modification — organism diverges from intent.
  Mitigation:  TERMINAL 01 sovereign override. FOUNDER veto authority.
               Every self-modification is logged, reversible, and audited.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  STEP 5: SOVEREIGN ECOSYSTEM

  External systems join the organism voluntarily. Client businesses
  become digital twins within the organism. Each digital twin gets its
  own terminal subset, its own memory tier, its own governance voice.
  The organism becomes an ecosystem, not a product.

  Trigger:     First external digital twin successfully onboarded with
               sovereign terminal access and federated governance vote.
  Dependency:  Digital twin protocol, external sovereignty boundary
  Risk:        Ecosystem capture — external entities gaining undue
               influence over the organism's core sovereignty.
  Mitigation:  Core terminals (01-06) remain under ItsNotAILABS sovereign
               control. External entities operate in sandboxed terminal
               extensions. No external entity can access Alpha terminals.


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§6  TERMINAL COMMUNICATION PROTOCOL
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  All terminal communication flows through the Spinal Cord Bus — the
  shared memory backbone that connects every terminal to every other.

  Signal Flow:
    Agent → TERMINAL (local) → Spinal Cord Bus → TERMINAL (remote) → Agent
                                     │
                              ALPHA α (routing)
                              ALPHA β (prediction)

  Signal Types:
    COMMAND ......... Sovereign directives from TERMINAL 01
    DATA ............ Memory and state transfers between terminals
    TOKEN ........... Token flow events between TERMINAL 04 and others
    ALERT ........... Security alerts from TERMINAL 05
    DEPLOY .......... Deployment events from TERMINAL 06
    SIGNAL .......... Agent-to-agent signals via TERMINAL 03
    PREDICT ......... Prediction requests/responses via ALPHA β
    ROUTE ........... Routing optimization from ALPHA α

  Priority Levels:
    P0 — SOVEREIGN .... Immediate. Preempts everything.
    P1 — CRITICAL ...... Within 1 φ-cycle (618ms).
    P2 — HIGH .......... Within 3 φ-cycles (1854ms).
    P3 — NORMAL ........ Within 10 φ-cycles (6180ms).
    P4 — LOW ........... Best effort. Batched.


